     5	`_handle_frame` — there is no REST approval route):
     6	
     7	  create_build_conversation  POST /conversations (surface="build")    conversations.py:49
     8	                             + POST /conversations/{cid}/messages      conversations.py:149
     9	                               (appends the user message AND kicks the loop)
    10	  approve_plan               WS {"type":"approve_plan"}    ws.py:95 -> runtime.approve_plan
    11	  confirm                    WS {"type":"confirm"}         ws.py:89 -> runtime.confirm (clears
    12	                             WAITING_FOR_CONFIRMATION — the confirm analogue of approve_plan;
    13	                             a plain message does NOT clear this gate)
    14	  send_followup (message)    WS {"type":"send_message"}    ws.py:48 -> store.append + kick
    15	  send_followup (steer)      WS {"type":"steer",...}       ws.py:58 -> store.append(steer)+kick
    16	  send_followup (request_plan) WS {"type":"request_plan"}  ws.py:98 -> runtime.request_plan
    17	  poll_until_terminal        GET /conversations/{cid}/state           conversations.py:195
    18	  collect_events             read disco.db DIRECTLY, post-terminal (race-free; trace pattern)
    19	  collect_state              GET /conversations/{cid}/state
    20	  collect_workspace          read the host ProjectStore SNAPSHOT directly (authoritative;
    21	                             Bug 9 fix — NOT the dev-server preview proxy, which 404s when
    22	                             the served app isn't up). Falls back to the preview proxy.
    23	  collect_preview            GET /conversations/{cid}/preview + preview-app root
    24	
    25	approve_plan / request_plan are WS-ONLY (there is no REST approval route — verified
    26	in routes/conversations.py: only create/messages/followup/events/state/kill/resume
    27	exist; the plan gate lives in routes/ws.py `_handle_frame`). So the runner opens a
    28	short-lived WS, sends the one control frame, and closes — the loop state machine
    29	acts on it; truth is read back from the durable event log / state.
    30	
    31	INFRA GATE (codex #3): `pre_create_probe` is the ONLY place INFRA_FAILURE can come
    32	from — a RUNNER-SIDE failure BEFORE a conversation_id exists. A post-create
    33	driver-preflight error surfaces as StatusEvent(ERROR) AFTER the user event and is a
    34	PRODUCT outcome (classified by the oracle), never infra.
    35	
    36	Transport is abstracted so the deterministic tests can drive the orchestration with
    37	a FAKE transport (no live model spend); the live path uses HttpTransport (httpx +
    38	websockets) behind the opt-in `@pytest.mark.live` marker.
    39	"""
